Z||000000||Pheagey||11-14-2001||02:17 PM||||   Ok, I got the steering wheel off but how do I get the lock plate off?...Thanks<P>------------------<BR>'88 coupe, 5 speed.<BR>"The car formerly know as Dj's Fiero."||||reg||1
Z||000001||PBJ||11-14-2001||02:47 PM||||special tool that threads on steering shaft and is u shaped that pushes down on lock plate so you can access and see retaining clip/ring.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Pheagey||11-14-2001||02:55 PM||||na na. I got the steering wheel itself off (using the special tool). Do I have to use the same tool to get ther lock plate off also?||||reg||1
Z||000003||mrfiero||11-14-2001||03:11 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Pheagey:<BR><B>na na. I got the steering wheel itself off (using the special tool). Do I have to use the same tool to get ther lock plate off also?</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Different tool. Go to a parts store and ask for a lockplate remover tool......they'll know what it is.  El cheapos are around $10, so it's not an expensive thing to buy.<P>What this tool does is push in on the lockplate (there is a spring in the other side) so you can see/remove the small ring that holds the lockplate to the column shaft.  You'll need a small flat blade screwdriver or something similar to pry off the old ring.  If you don't bend it up too much it can be reused.<P>When you remove the ring and remove the tool the lockplate and some things behind it will come off.  Make sure you see how/where everything goes before completely removing.  <P>Other than that it's pretty straightforward.<P>Good luck! <BR>     ||||reg||1
